message.reasoning_content:
Let me write solutions for all 17 tasks in Ailullai.

Key things to remember:
- `plulai name := expr;` declares
- `shaibe expr;` prints with newline
- `yaiyoyai expr;` prints without newline
- Functions: `nudro name(params) { ... }`
- Loops: `edrushe cond ... aitudre`, `shoyo i := a busale b ede s ... pla i`, `enala ... beldra yelrol cond`
- `atdoyal (cond) { } bulbayo atdoyal (cond) { } bulbayo { }`
- `otai (expr) { dai lit: ... efu: ... }`
- Input: `playadu()` reads a line, returns `aiplai` at EOF
- `dra(x)` length of list/string
- `sutse(xs, v)` appends
- `plaltai(s)` string to int
- `sutlarai(v)` value to string
- Strings indexable, lists mutable, `x[i] := v`
- Integer division rounds toward zero; `%` follows sign of a.
- No while loop break other than `sonaidel`, `deye`.
- No else-if chains need `bulbayo atdoyal`.

Now tasks.

### t0-count
Read n, print 1..n.

```
plulai n := plaltai(playadu());
shoyo i := 1 busale n
    shaibe i;
pla i
```
n=0 prints nothing since loop runs while not passed limit: start 1 > 0, no iterations. Good.

### t0-day-names
Read lines until aiplai. For each integer print day name.

```
plulai line := playadu();
edrushe line <> aiplai
    plulai n := plaltai(line);
    atdoyal (n == 1) { shaibe "Monday"; }
    bulbayo atdoyal (n == 2) { shaibe "Tuesday"; }
    ... 
    bulbayo { shaibe "invalid"; }
    line := playadu();
aitudre
```
Could use otai. otai works on literals: `otai (n) { dai 1: ... efu: ... }`. Cleaner.

### t0-even-odd
Count evens/odds. Negative numbers: n % 2 in Ailullai: -7 % 2 = -1, so check `n % 2 == 0` for even.

```
plulai e := 0; plulai o := 0;
plulai line := playadu();
edrushe line <> aiplai
    plulai n := plaltai(line);
    atdoyal (n % 2 == 0) { e := e + 1; } bulbayo { o := o + 1; }
    line := playadu();
aitudre
shaibe "even " + e;
shaibe "odd " + o;
```

