### t1-digits
Sum of digits, reversed digits as integer keeping sign.

For n=0: "0 0". For -4500: digit sum 9 (4+5+0+0), reversed -54 (reverse digits "0054" -> integer 54, keep sign).

Approach: work with string of absolute value. s = sutlarai(n); if starts with '-', strip. Then iterate chars, sum digit values, build reversed string, then plaltai reversed (leading zeros dropped automatically by plaltai? "plaltai converts a string of decimal digits to integer" — leading zeros? It says anything else is error; "054" is decimal digits so returns 54 presumably. Safer to strip leading zeros manually or just plaltai — should be fine.)

Sum: iterate over chars of abs string. Reversed: build string from end.

```
plulai line := playadu();
edrushe line <> aiplai
    plulai n := plaltai(line);
    plulai neg := n < 0;
    plulai s := sutlarai(n);
    atdoyal (neg) {
        s := ... substring?
    }
```
No substring builtin. But we can index: s[i] one-char string. Build abs string by looping i from (neg?1:0) to dra(s)-1, appending chars.

Digit char to int: plaltai(char).

Reversed: iterate from end to start of abs string, append chars, then plaltai handles leading zeros. Check plaltai("0054") => 54. The spec: "converts a string of decimal digits (optionally with a leading -, surrounding spaces allowed) to an integer." I think leading zeros fine.

n=0: s="0", abs="0", sum=0, rev="0", plaltai=>0. Good. Sign only if neg (n<0); 0 not negative. -0? Input integer won't be -0 presumably; plaltai("-0") = 0, n<0 false, fine.

```
plulai line := playadu();
edrushe line <> aiplai
    plulai n := plaltai(line);
    plulai s := sutlarai(n);
    plulai start := 0;
    atdoyal (n < 0) { start := 1; }
    plulai sum := 0;
    plulai rev := "";
    plulai i := start;
    edrushe i < dra(s)
        sum := sum + plaltai(s[i]);
        i := i + 1;
    aitudre
    plulai j := dra(s) - 1;
    edrushe j >= start
        rev := rev + s[j];
        j := j - 1;
    aitudre
    plulai r := plaltai(rev);
    atdoyal (n < 0) { r := -r; }
    shaibe sum + " " + r;
    line := playadu();
aitudre
```

### t1-floordiv
Need floor division (toward -inf) and r = a - b*q with sign of b.

Ailullai / rounds toward zero. Compute q0 = a / b, r0 = a % b. If r0 != 0 and signs of r0 and b differ (i.e., (a<0) <> (b<0) and r0 !=0), then floor q = q0 - 1, r = a - b*q. Actually simpler: if r0 != 0 and ((a < 0) <> (b < 0)), q = q0 - 1; else q = q0. Then r = a - b*q.

Example: -7 2: q0=-3, r0=-1, signs differ → q=-4, r = -7 -2*-4 = 1. Good.
7 -2: q0=-3, r0=1, signs of a and b differ → q=-4, r=7 - (-2)(-4) = 7-8 = -1. Good.
7 2: q=3 r=1. -7 -2: q0=3, r0=-1, signs same (both neg) → q=3, r=-7-(-2*3)=-7+6=-1. r sign of b (negative) good.

### t1-palindromes
Keep letters/digits, lowercase, check palindrome.

Build filtered string: for each char c in line, if (c >= "a" and c <= "z") or (c >= "A" and c <= "Z") or digit, then lowercase letters. Lowercase: map A-Z to a-z. No char-code builtin! Need to map via comparisons. For uppercase letter c, lowercase = ... We can use otai on the character? 26 cases — tedious but works. Alternatively compare and construct: since strings compare char by code, we can do arithmetic on... no char arithmetic.

Alternative: use otai with dai for each uppercase letter mapping to lowercase. That's 26 dai clauses, each with literal string. That's allowed: literals are integers or strings. Let me write a helper function lower(c) using chained atdoyal or otai.

Actually simpler: use otai:
```
nudro lower(c) {
    otai (c) {
    dai "A": druna "a";
    dai "B": druna "b";
    ...
    efu: druna c;
    }
}
```
Wait — `druna` inside otai case: allowed, statements in case. But does `sonaidel` note imply you can leave early; normal execution of a case: does it fall through? Reference says "Only the statements of the matching case run (or the default part if none matches); execution does not continue into the next case." So no fall-through. But `druna` returns anyway.

Then check palindrome on filtered string t: compare t[i] vs t[dra(t)-1-i].

```
nudro lower(c) { ... }

plulai line := playadu();
edrushe line <> aiplai
    plulai t := "";
    plulai i := 0;
    edrushe i < dra(line)
        plulai c := line[i];
        atdoyal ((c >= "a" rulsai c <= "z") ... 
```
Careful: conditions must be booleans. `(c >= "a") rulsai (c <= "z")`. Letters/digits condition:
```
plulai ok := (c >= "a" rulsai c <= "z"); # wait precedence: comparisons bind tighter than rulsai. c >= "a" rulsai c <= "z" parses as (c>="a") rulsai (c<="z"). good.
```
Actually simpler: `atdoyal (c >= "0" rulsai c <= "9") { ... } bulbayo atdoyal (c >= "a" rulsai c <= "z") { ... } bulbayo atdoyal (c >= "A" rulsai c <= "Z") { t := t + lower(c); }`.

Then palindrome check with two pointers or compare with reversed. Build rev and compare equality.

Empty filtered: palindrome yes.

